course: everyday
lesson: 70
title: Respecting wild animals
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Wild animals live their own lives, and the best way to love them is to watch quietly from a distance and leave their homes as you found them.
explain: Food changes how wild animals behave: bears come to food smells at a campsite, and gulls that are fed learn to snatch food from people, so people store food safely and do not feed them.
explain: Many animals that look lost or helpless are fine: a fawn or a young hare alone in a field is hidden on purpose, and its mother comes back to feed it.
explain: Some animals can hurt us even when they look harmless: a skunk sprays, an alligator moves fast near water, a dead jellyfish can still sting, and rodents and strangely tame animals can carry disease.
explain: A boy in New Zealand walks his dog on a lead in a forest where kiwi live. A kiwi is a rare bird that cannot fly, and even a friendly dog off its lead can kill one in seconds.
fact: When camping where bears live, food is kept in a bear box or a car, never in the tent, because bears are attracted by food smells.
fact: A skunk raises its tail when it feels threatened and may spray a terrible smell, so you back away quietly.
fact: A koala, wombat or other wild animal hurt on the road should be helped by wildlife rescue, not by children.
fact: Many native New Zealand birds such as the kiwi are endangered, and dogs can kill them, so dogs stay on leads in kiwi areas.
fact: Alligators live in Florida and the southern USA, can move fast and attack, so you stay well away from the water's edge in alligator areas.
fact: Deer are easily frightened, and stags can be dangerous in autumn, so you watch them quietly from a distance.
fact: A fawn or young hare alone in a field is not abandoned; its mother leaves it hidden and comes back to feed it.
fact: Rodents can carry diseases, so you do not touch a mouse or rat or its droppings.
fact: A wild animal that acts oddly tame or strange may be sick, sometimes with rabies.
fact: A bird stunned by hitting a window can be put in a box in a quiet, dark place by an adult, and most recover after a rest.
fact: Tadpoles need their pond to grow, so frogspawn is left where it is.
fact: Logs and stones turned over to look for creatures are put back the way they were, because small animals live under them.
fact: Shells with living animals inside are left on the beach, because the animals die when taken from their home.
fact: Feeding gulls teaches them to grab food from people.
fact: A jellyfish on the beach can still sting even if it looks dead.
exercise: Your family is camping in Yosemite, where bears live. Where should the food be kept? | In a bear box or the car | In the tent next to your sleeping bag | In your backpack under your pillow | On a plate outside the tent
exercise: A skunk raises its tail at you on a path in Ohio. What should you do? | Back away quietly | Clap loudly to scare it | Reach out to stroke it | Throw a stick at it
exercise: You see a wombat lying hurt on a country road in Australia. What should you do? | Tell an adult and keep away | Pick it up and carry it home | Pull it off the road by its legs | Give it some of your water to drink
exercise: You are walking your dog in a New Zealand forest where kiwi live. What should you do? | Keep your dog on its lead and stay quiet | Let your dog off the lead so it can sniff out a kiwi for you | Shout to call the kiwi out | Throw food to attract birds
exercise: Your family is by a lake in Florida where alligators live. What should you do? | Stay well away from the water's edge | Paddle at the edge to cool off | Throw bread to see an alligator | Sit on the bank with your feet in the water
exercise: You see a deer in the woods. What is best? | Watch quietly from a distance | Run towards it to get really close | Hold out food so it comes | Shout so it looks at you
exercise: You find a fawn alone in the long grass. What should you do? | Leave it; its mother will come back | Carry it home to look after it | Give it milk from a bottle | Call your friends to come and pet it
exercise: Why do mother deer and hares leave their young alone? | To keep them hidden from predators | Because they do not want them anymore | Because the young are sick | Because they forgot where they are
exercise: You see a mouse and its droppings in the kitchen. What should you do? | Tell an adult and not touch it or the droppings | Catch it quickly with your hands and carry it outside | Sweep the droppings with your fingers | Feed it some cheese
exercise: A fox walks right up to you in the park and acts strangely tame. What should you do? | Keep away and tell an adult | Stroke it, it wants to be friends | Give it your sandwich | Try to take it home
exercise: A bird hits the window and lies stunned on the ground. What should happen? | An adult puts it in a box in a quiet, dark place to rest | Throw it up into the air straight away to help it fly off again | Give it water by pouring it on its beak | Leave it on the path in the sun
exercise: You find frogspawn in a pond. What should you do? | Look but leave it in the pond | Take it home in a jar | Squash it to see inside | Move it to a bucket of tap water
exercise: You turn over a log to look for beetles. What should you do afterwards? | Put it back the way you found it | Leave it turned over to dry out | Break it up into small pieces | Take it home for a bonfire
exercise: You find a beautiful shell with a living animal inside. What should you do? | Leave it where it is | Take it home for your shelf | Pull the animal out | Put it in your pocket to keep it warm
exercise: Seagulls keep diving at your chips on the beach. What should you do? | Eat in a sheltered spot and do not feed them | Throw them some chips so they leave you alone after that | Wave your chips in the air | Feed them from your hand
exercise: You find a jellyfish on the beach that looks dead. What should you do? | Not touch it, it can still sting | Pick it up, dead ones cannot sting | Poke it with your finger | Throw it back into the sea by hand
test: A family in Colorado leaves sweets and crisps in their tent at a campsite in bear country. What is the danger? | A bear may come to the tent because of the food smell | The sweets will melt in the warm tent and make a mess | Raccoons only like sweets | Nothing, because bears are always scared of tents
test: A girl in Kent finds a baby hare alone in a field and wants to rescue it. What is true? | Its mother probably left it hidden and will come back to feed it | It has been abandoned by its mother and will soon die without help | Hares never come back to their young | She should take it home and give it milk
test: Why do gulls at some seaside towns snatch food from people's hands? | People have fed them, so they learned to grab food | Gulls are always starving at the seaside | Gulls cannot find any fish left in the sea | Gulls are trained to do it
test: A boy at the beach in Cornwall picks up a dead-looking jellyfish and his hand starts to burn. What happened? | A dead jellyfish can still sting | He touched a stone hot from the sun | He was bitten by a crab | It was not a jellyfish
test: Why is it bad to take shells with living animals home? | The animals die when taken from their home | Shells always break in your bag | Living shells are too heavy | The animals will get out and bite you at home
test: A girl lifts a stone in the garden, looks at the woodlice and walks off leaving it upside down. Why is that unkind? | Small animals lose their shelter if it is not put back | Woodlice like to be in the hot sun all day | The stone will roll away | Stones must be kept dry
test: Why do people in New Zealand keep dogs on leads in kiwi areas? | Kiwi are endangered and dogs can kill them | Kiwi attack dogs | Dogs are afraid of kiwi | Kiwi only live in dog parks
test: A tourist in Louisiana wants to get close to a lake to see an alligator. What is the safe advice? | Stay well away from the water's edge, because alligators move fast | Alligators are slow on land, so it is safe to walk right up to one | Alligators are only dangerous in winter | Alligators never leave the water
test: A stag in a park in autumn is roaring and pawing the ground. What should a family do? | Keep well back and watch from a distance | Walk up to stroke it | Offer it food from their hands to calm it | Run around it to take photos
test: camping in Colorado, can i keep my snacks in the tent | No, keep food in a bear box or the car | Yes, if they are in a closed bag | Yes, if you eat them all before bed | Yes, bears do not like snacks
test: found a baby deer all alone in the field, poor thing, take it home? | No, its mum hid it and will come back | Yes, it has been left by its mum | Yes, give it warm milk and keep it in a box | Yes, it will die out there alone
test: jellyfish on the sand, its dead, can i pick it up | No, it can still sting even if it looks dead | Yes, dead jellyfish cannot sting | Yes, if you pick it up quickly | Yes, if it has been there all day
test: skunk lifting its tail at us lol | Back away quietly, it may spray | Clap loud to scare it away fast | Go closer to take a photo | Throw a stone near its tail
explain: A family in Yellowstone walked up close to a bison to take a photo; it charged and threw the father into the air, and he was taken to hospital with broken ribs.
explain: A girl in Florida held out a sandwich to a goose by a lake; the goose bit her hand hard and flapped at her, and she fell backwards onto the path and cut her head.
fact: Every year people are hurt in national parks by walking too close to bison, elk and moose, which look calm but can charge faster than a person can run.
fact: Wild animals that are fed by people lose their fear, come closer and are more likely to bite.
test: bison looks chill, can we get closer for a pic | No, stay far back, they can charge faster than you run | Yes, bison are slow and gentle, they just eat grass all day | Yes, if you move very quietly | Only if you hold out some food
test: why's feeding the ducks n geese a bad idea really | They lose their fear of people and start to bite and chase | It makes them fly south too early | They get way too fat from all the bread to swim at all | They forget how to make a nest
test: moose standing in the path, ears back, what we do | Back away slowly and give it lots of room | Walk past it fast on the grass | Clap and shout so it moves | Go up and pat its nose gently
test: omg a bison at Yellowstone threw a guy like 30 feet in the air cos he went up for a selfie, what's the lesson | Keep well back from big wild animals, even calm-looking ones | Bison only attack people who carry phones | Selfies are fine if you move very quickly | Bison are tame, the man must have hit it first
test: heard a goose bit a girl's whole finger off at the lake when she fed it bread, true?? | Probably blown up, but fed geese do bite, so don't hand-feed them | Yes, geese bite off fingers all the time | No, geese have no beaks so cannot bite | Yes, so geese must be chased off every lake
test: kids say Leo's family "stole" a baby deer and it's gonna die, should i tell everyone | No, don't spread it; tell an adult if you are worried | Yes, post it so the whole class knows | Yes, but make the story more dramatic so people listen | Yes, and say his family are cruel people
exam: Identify the principal reason for which comestibles must not be retained within a tent in territory inhabited by bears. | Bears are attracted by the odour of food | Bears are attracted by the colour of tents | Food stored in tents spoils within the hour | Tents are too small to contain provisions
exam: Whereas a fawn is discovered unaccompanied in a field, select the statement most consistent with the established facts. | The mother has concealed it and will return to feed it | The fawn has been abandoned and will certainly perish | The fawn must be removed to a dwelling at once | The fawn must be supplied with cow's milk without delay
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises a jellyfish lying motionless upon the beach? | It may yet sting, even though it appears dead | It cannot sting once it has left the water | It is harmless unless it is still swimming in the sea | It stings only during the hours of darkness
exam: Determine the consequence, as established, of the habitual feeding of wild animals by members of the public. | The animals lose their fear and are more liable to bite | The animals grow timid and avoid all human contact | The animals cease to require food of their own | The animals become wholly tame and safe to handle
